# Cloudflare Pages header rules.
#
# Force the correct MIME on the PWA web app manifest — some browsers reject
# the install prompt when Content-Type isn't application/manifest+json.
/manifest.webmanifest
  Content-Type: application/manifest+json

# Long-cache Vite's hashed asset bundles. The name carries a content hash, so
# a year is safe for anything that actually exists.
#
# Deliberately NO `cdn-cache-control` here. Pages answers a missing asset with
# `cache-control: no-store`, which keeps Cloudflare from caching that 404 at
# all — so the deploy-propagation race (a PoP asked for a chunk seconds before
# it had the new version) self-heals on the very next request. An earlier
# revision set `cdn-cache-control: max-age=600` as a safety net and got the
# opposite: it overrode the no-store and pinned those 404s on the edge for ten
# minutes, breaking the site on that PoP for exactly as long.
#
# The safety net is no longer needed anyway: what made 2026-08-18 a 20-hour
# outage was a *200* — the SPA catch-all answering an /assets/*.js request with
# index.html, which then cached for a year. There is no catch-all any more
# (see public/_redirects), so the only cacheable response left under this path
# is a real file.
/assets/*
  cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# NO cache-control rule for /sw.js here, and not for lack of trying.
#
# Pages answers it with `public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate` and keeps
# doing so whatever this file says: a rule of
#   /sw.js
#     cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
#     x-fb-headers-rule: sw
# deployed on 2026-08-27 put `x-fb-headers-rule` on the response and left
# cache-control untouched — the rule matches, Pages just wins on that header.
# (The /assets/* line above survives because it happens to agree with what
# Pages already does for hashed bundles.)
#
# It does not bite in practice, which is why there is no workaround: a browser
# re-fetching sw.js bypasses its own HTTP cache by spec, and a Pages deploy
# invalidates the edge copy — the new worker was live on the PoP seconds after
# the deploy that added this comment.
